00:17:55.830 --> 00:17:58.350
<v Speaker 2>protecting it from insects, from bugs.

186
00:18:00.430 --> 00:18:00.750
<v Speaker 2>Right?

187
00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:04.910
<v Speaker 2>So in other words, Tarbiya is a bunch of do's and don'ts.

188
00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:13.070
<v Speaker 2>It's a bunch of do's and don'ts that apply until the plant matures into a tree and starts bearing fruit.

189
00:18:13.790 --> 00:18:14.350
<v Speaker 2>You understand?

190
00:18:15.550 --> 00:18:18.750
<v Speaker 2>If you just leave the plant to grow by itself, that's not Tarbiya.

191
00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:21.870
<v Speaker 2>Because you're not setting limits, you're not setting boundaries.

192
00:18:23.910 --> 00:18:27.790
<v Speaker 2>Similarly, Tarbiya of children is taking care of their physical needs.

193
00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:34.110
<v Speaker 2>Their diets, their clothing, their mental needs, educating them, protecting them.

194
00:18:35.470 --> 00:18:40.430
<v Speaker 2>It's a bunch of do's and don'ts that are applied to a child for their nurturing.

195
00:18:42.430 --> 00:18:49.470
<v Speaker 2>Tarbiya is basically monitoring their progress from birth until they mature into a healthy, sound, decent human being.

196
00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:57.470
<v Speaker 2>From a physical point of view, from a psychological point of view, from a social point of view, that's all tarbiyatul awlad.

197
00:19:01.390 --> 00:19:04.510
<v Speaker 2>Now, is tarbiyya a quick and easy process?

198
00:19:05.950 --> 00:19:09.790
<v Speaker 2>Or is it a very long, very time-consuming process?

199
00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:16.270
<v Speaker 2>It is very hard work, isn't it?

200
00:19:16.270 --> 00:19:18.670
<v Speaker 2>In fact, it is the most difficult process there is.

201
00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:23.710
<v Speaker 2>It's a never-ending, thankless, full-time job.

202
00:19:28.910 --> 00:19:29.230
<v Speaker 2>Right?

203
00:19:29.550 --> 00:19:37.950
<v Speaker 2>And parents do that and this is why when we recite at-tahiyat in every prayer, what do we say at the end of at-tahiyat?

204
00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:41.470
<v Speaker 2>We say, Allahum maqfir li O Allah forgive me.

205
00:19:41.750 --> 00:19:44.670
<v Speaker 2>Wa li walidaiya and my parents.

206
00:19:48.110 --> 00:19:56.430
<v Speaker 2>And have mercy on them just as they had mercy on me during my period of Tarbiyya when I was young.

207
00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:00.350
<v Speaker 2>Right?

208
00:20:00.590 --> 00:20:06.750
<v Speaker 2>And this is such a beautiful statement because in this statement we find the primary ingredient of Rububiyya.

209
00:20:08.510 --> 00:20:09.870
<v Speaker 2>Allahum maghfili

210
00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:22.750
<v Speaker 2>Forgive me, forgive my parents and have mercy on them just as they had mercy on me.

211
00:20:24.110 --> 00:20:31.870
<v Speaker 2>So what we're learning is that the primary ingredient of Rububiya, of Tarbiya, of nurturing is mercy.

212
00:20:34.110 --> 00:20:36.990
<v Speaker 2>The primary component of Rububiya is mercy.

213
00:20:37.790 --> 00:20:40.750
<v Speaker 2>If there is no mercy, then there can be no Rububiya.

214
00:20:43.790 --> 00:20:44.750
<v Speaker 2>And what is mercy?

215
00:20:44.910 --> 00:20:48.430
<v Speaker 2>It is a combination of love, care, and concern.

216
00:20:50.190 --> 00:20:52.790
<v Speaker 2>It's a combination of love, care, and concern.

217
00:20:52.790 --> 00:20:54.270
<v Speaker 2>That's what rububia is made of.

218
00:20:55.710 --> 00:21:00.750
<v Speaker 2>Have you ever seen people, you know, who shower all this TLC on plants?

219
00:21:00.750 --> 00:21:06.550
<v Speaker 2>Like they'll actually spray each individual leaf with water and then clean them gently with a soft cloth.

220
00:21:07.550 --> 00:21:07.790
<v Speaker 2>Right?

221
00:21:07.790 --> 00:21:10.350
<v Speaker 2>That kind of love and that kind of care

222
00:21:11.150 --> 00:21:14.110
<v Speaker 2>with that level of gentleness is imamate of Rububiyya.

223
00:21:15.470 --> 00:21:31.630
<v Speaker 2>It is not harshness, it is not beating, it is not you know psychological, emotional, physical abuse, it is not guilt tripping into submission, it is love, it is patience, it is kindness.

224
00:21:32.830 --> 00:21:34.790
<v Speaker 2>That's the Islamic way of Rububiyya.

225
00:21:35.950 --> 00:21:39.310
<v Speaker 2>I mean this is contrary to what we see in movies and society today

226
00:21:40.590 --> 00:21:51.350
<v Speaker 2>you know but the reality is that the Islamic way of Rububiya has always been Rahmah, it has always been mercy and the proof of that is the life of Rasulullah sallallahu alaihi wasallam.

227
00:21:51.790 --> 00:22:06.670
<v Speaker 2>I mean just think about this right, our Prophet sallallahu alaihi wasallam he took the most vicious people, he took the most barbaric people and without a single slap, without a single taunt

228
00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:23.990
<v Speaker 2>without a single sarcastic remark, without a single insult, he turned them into Radi Allahu Anhu, he turned them into Rahmatullah Alaihi, the greatest titles possible for a human being Radi Allah Anhu.

229
00:22:25.070 --> 00:22:32.190
<v Speaker 2>I mean we don't mention the name of these Sahaba without saying Radi Allah Anhu, without saying Rahmatullah Alaihi and our Prophet did that.

230
00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:35.070
<v Speaker 2>How?

231
00:22:36.350 --> 00:22:42.910
<v Speaker 2>with his love, with his gentleness, with his kindness, forgiveness, patience, with his akhlaq.

232
00:22:44.510 --> 00:22:45.550
<v Speaker 2>That's Rububiyya.

233
00:22:49.870 --> 00:22:53.430
<v Speaker 2>And sometimes harshness is necessary in Rububiyya.

234
00:22:53.430 --> 00:22:54.750
<v Speaker 2>There's no question about that.

235
00:22:55.310 --> 00:22:58.430
<v Speaker 2>But behind that harshness, the sentiment is still Rahma.

236
00:23:01.350 --> 00:23:04.830
<v Speaker 2>It's not frustration, it's not anger, it is still Rahma.

237
00:23:05.070 --> 00:23:07.950
<v Speaker 2>It is to guide the person.

238
00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:22.430
<v Speaker 2>Because you have tried love, you have tried kindness, it's not working but you have to make sure that this person does the right thing so now you resort to harshness but even behind that harshness your primary ingredient is still love.

239
00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:35.070
<v Speaker 2>that is Raboobiyah and Rasulallah's life is the perfect example of that Raboobiyah and you know and what did he ask in return is very interesting.

240
00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:49.230
<v Speaker 2>Rasulallah after devoting 23 years of his life to our Tarbiyya, have you guys ever heard the last Khutba, the Hajjatul-Wida, the last sermon of the Prophet when he was returning from Hajj?

241
00:23:50.030 --> 00:23:51.870
<v Speaker 2>That is something really incredible.

242
00:23:52.350 --> 00:23:53.550
<v Speaker 2>In that he says

243
00:23:54.550 --> 00:24:09.390
<v Speaker 2>he says many things, but one of the things he says towards the end when he's ending his khutbah, he says, Ya ummati laa tusawwidawajhiyo malqiyama O my people, do not embarrass me before Allah on the Day of Judgment.

244
00:24:11.310 --> 00:24:12.510
<v Speaker 2>That's what he says in the end.

245
00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:18.190
<v Speaker 2>Ya ummati laa tusawwidawajiyo malqiyama Do not embarrass me before Allah on the Day of Judgment.

246
00:24:21.030 --> 00:24:23.550
<v Speaker 2>You know, when a result does not meet expectations,

247
00:24:24.590 --> 00:24:29.070
<v Speaker 2>If majority of the students fail in a class, doesn't the principal question the teacher?

248
00:24:29.630 --> 00:24:30.510
<v Speaker 2>What's going on?

249
00:24:30.990 --> 00:24:32.750
<v Speaker 2>Why are there so many failures in your class?

250
00:24:34.910 --> 00:24:43.550
<v Speaker 2>And our Prophet after 23 years of service and Tarbiya asked us for one thing in return.

251
00:24:47.390 --> 00:24:49.790
<v Speaker 2>Do not embarrass me before Allah on the Day of Judgment.

252
00:24:50.310 --> 00:24:54.670
<v Speaker 2>And so may Allah make us among those who makes our, you know, who make our Prophet proud on that day.

253
00:24:57.070 --> 00:24:58.830
<v Speaker 2>So that is Rububiyya, right?

254
00:24:58.830 --> 00:25:08.870
<v Speaker 2>And which is comes from the act of Rububiyya is Tarbiyya and the main ingredient of that Tarbiyya is love, care and concern which is Rahma, right?

255
00:25:12.830 --> 00:25:16.910
<v Speaker 2>The fourth meaning of the word Arab comes from Murshad.

256
00:25:18.510 --> 00:25:22.190
<v Speaker 2>He is the one who sets things in the right direction.

257
00:25:23.870 --> 00:25:28.030
<v Speaker 2>The one who sets us on the right path, puts us to proper use.

258
00:25:28.270 --> 00:25:29.230
<v Speaker 2>That's a Murshat.

259
00:25:31.230 --> 00:25:32.190
<v Speaker 2>The guide, right?

260
00:25:32.350 --> 00:25:37.870
<v Speaker 2>The word Murshat comes from the word Rushd, which in classical Arabic means to ripen.

261
00:25:39.910 --> 00:25:42.830
<v Speaker 2>You know when a fruit ripens, it's the final stage for a fruit.

262
00:25:43.550 --> 00:25:52.030
<v Speaker 2>That's why Rusht also means rationality, it also means reason, it also means maturity because those are the final stages of intellect.

263
00:25:54.510 --> 00:26:11.630
<v Speaker 2>So Murshad is the one who takes something in its raw form, works on it with Rahma, with love, continues working on it, it gets involved in its Tarbiya until it ripens, till it matures.

264
00:26:12.830 --> 00:26:15.470
<v Speaker 2>and then he puts it to proper use.

265
00:26:16.910 --> 00:26:18.990
<v Speaker 2>He sets it in the right direction.

266
00:26:18.990 --> 00:26:20.030
<v Speaker 2>This is a murshid.

267
00:26:20.510 --> 00:26:33.630
<v Speaker 2>So let's say if you have a teacher who trains you let's say in computer sciences and then also finds you a job so that you can now apply what you've learned that would be a murshid.

268
00:26:35.510 --> 00:26:39.150
<v Speaker 2>And a murshid is not someone who just trains you to maturity and then leaves you to rot.

269
00:26:41.390 --> 00:26:43.470
<v Speaker 2>He actually designs a path for you.

270
00:26:44.270 --> 00:26:47.710
<v Speaker 2>He guides you forward from your point of maturity.

271
00:26:47.990 --> 00:26:51.390
<v Speaker 2>You know he provides you with the opportunity to practice what you've learned.

272
00:26:52.750 --> 00:26:56.190
<v Speaker 2>So that you and others can benefit from what you've learned.

273
00:26:56.430 --> 00:26:57.470
<v Speaker 2>That's a mushad.

274
00:27:01.150 --> 00:27:01.790
<v Speaker 2>You understand?

275
00:27:03.390 --> 00:27:09.230
<v Speaker 2>So what we're learning here is that Allah as Rabb is not only someone who owns us.

276
00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:25.230
<v Speaker 2>who has complete authority over us, who nurtures us with love, care and concern but he is also the one to create opportunities for us so that we can put our training to use.

277
00:27:30.410 --> 00:27:35.930
<v Speaker 2>He trains us by putting people in our path that will help us grow and develop.

278
00:27:38.250 --> 00:27:40.730
<v Speaker 2>He's the one who find the perfect friend for you.

279
00:27:42.090 --> 00:27:44.730
<v Speaker 2>That friend who is going to help you grow and mature.

280
00:27:45.210 --> 00:27:49.690
<v Speaker 2>And then he will use that friend to guide you in putting that training to use.

281
00:27:52.570 --> 00:27:55.930
<v Speaker 2>By becoming good friends to other people.

282
00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:57.770
<v Speaker 2>That's what a mushad does.

283
00:28:00.490 --> 00:28:03.370
<v Speaker 2>And this is something even parents and teachers cannot do at times.

284
00:28:03.410 --> 00:28:09.130
<v Speaker 2>You know, they will complete the tarbiyah of their children but then they cannot always put them to proper use.

285
00:28:09.290 --> 00:28:10.850
<v Speaker 2>You know, kids have to find their own path.

286
00:28:11.770 --> 00:28:19.930
<v Speaker 2>But Allah as Rabb, He goes the extra mile and He sets us on the right path after our training has been completed.

287
00:28:20.730 --> 00:28:22.130
<v Speaker 2>That's a mushad, subhanallah.

288
00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:25.770
<v Speaker 2>Alright, so that's the fourth meaning.

289
00:28:28.330 --> 00:28:32.090
<v Speaker 2>The next meaning comes from the word Munahim.

290
00:28:33.210 --> 00:28:40.730
<v Speaker 2>The word Munahim is used for the one who bestows gifts, the one who gives blessings.

291
00:28:40.890 --> 00:28:46.810
<v Speaker 2>It comes from the root word Niham or Nihamah which means gift or blessing.

292
00:28:49.450 --> 00:28:54.490
<v Speaker 2>We don't have slavery today but imagine for a second that if we owned a slave

293
00:28:56.010 --> 00:28:56.290
<v Speaker 2>Right?

294
00:28:56.570 --> 00:29:11.770
<v Speaker 2>Now it is one thing to own a slave and it is an entirely different thing to take care of that slave, to nurture that slave, to put that slave to proper use and then on top of everything bestow gifts upon the slave.

295
00:29:16.170 --> 00:29:22.170
<v Speaker 2>And the word Ni'amah suggests gifts and benefits that are on top of what he deserves.

296
00:29:23.290 --> 00:29:25.570
<v Speaker 2>Remember gifts are not deserved things.

297
00:29:26.250 --> 00:29:28.730
<v Speaker 2>Gift is not something you deserve.

298
00:29:29.130 --> 00:29:33.610
<v Speaker 2>Gift by definition you know they're always beyond what is deserved.

299
00:29:35.370 --> 00:29:36.170
<v Speaker 2>Isn't that true?

300
00:29:37.130 --> 00:29:40.570
<v Speaker 2>It is an expression of the love and mercy of the benefactor.

301
00:29:42.730 --> 00:29:45.610
<v Speaker 2>If you deserved it then that is salary.

302
00:29:45.850 --> 00:29:46.730
<v Speaker 2>That is a pay.

303
00:29:46.730 --> 00:29:47.610
<v Speaker 2>That is an earning.

304
00:29:47.770 --> 00:29:48.650
<v Speaker 2>It's not a gift.

305
00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:50.090
<v Speaker 2>You understand the difference?

306
00:29:54.170 --> 00:30:00.410
<v Speaker 2>Now interestingly the Arabic word for 'Naam' which means 'yes' also comes from the same root word as 'blessing'.

307
00:30:01.290 --> 00:30:01.610
<v Speaker 2>Why?

308
00:30:02.730 --> 00:30:05.850
<v Speaker 2>Because what's the natural human response to receiving blessing?

309
00:30:06.730 --> 00:30:07.930
<v Speaker 2>It's 'Naam' 'yes'.

310
00:30:08.330 --> 00:30:08.530
<v Speaker 2>Right?

311
00:30:08.530 --> 00:30:10.250
<v Speaker 2>Isn't that interesting?

312
00:30:11.330 --> 00:30:12.730
<v Speaker 2>And this is why I love this language.

313
00:30:13.050 --> 00:30:15.370
<v Speaker 2>Every word is so perfectly connected.

314
00:30:15.530 --> 00:30:18.890
<v Speaker 2>Every variation, every derivative is so perfectly connected to its root.

315
00:30:20.290 --> 00:30:23.530
<v Speaker 2>Anyway, so now imagine that you own a slave or an animal.

